WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.141 --> 00:00:02.100
(soft music)

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:05.740
- I'm from small West
African country, Liberia.

00:00:08.570 --> 00:00:11.510
And because of the civil war,

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:13.160
my wife and I fled Liberia.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:17.260
We had no intentions of leaving.

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:22.110
But when the war came, we
decided we had to survive.

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:26.070
And that's how we started that process

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:27.270
to come to this country.

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:31.990
I'm Wilmot Collins, and I'm the mayor

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:34.591
of the city of Helena, Montana.

00:00:34.591 --> 00:00:37.008
(soft music)

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:45.440
- [Narrator] When Wilmot
Collins gained refugee status

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:47.810
and moved to the United States in 1994,

00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:51.320
he came to Helena, Montana,
a town of about 30,000 people

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:53.770
that doesn't see a lot of change.

00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:55.040
More than two decades later,

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:57.170
he decided to run for mayor of Helena,

00:00:57.170 --> 00:01:00.200
challenging a popular 16-year
incumbent. When I became

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:03.800
- American, I said, "I
wanted run for office."

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:06.010
But as time will come closer and closer,

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:09.070
I got scared 'cause I
didn't know the processes.

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:11.070
I wasn't trained to be a mayor.

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:12.630
Come on, guys, let's eat.

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:15.950
Last year, my son came home and said,

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:17.840
"Dad, we need to talk."

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:19.700
"Your time is now."

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:21.300
"You have to run."

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:23.050
He pushed me and pushed me,

00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:25.770
and finally I said,
"OK, I'm going to run."

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:29.100
And I didn't run on a refugee platform.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:32.964
I ran on a platform that
would serve my community.

00:01:32.964 --> 00:01:34.130
Hi, how are you?

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:37.670
I met with people, I participated in every

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:41.220
political public forum that was held.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:43.700
I didn't shy away because I knew

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:46.450
the hard questions had to be answered.

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:47.620
- [Narrator] In Helena, Montana,

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:50.590
a first time candidate
beat a 16-year incumbent.

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:52.490
- [Female Reporter] The
first African-American mayor

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:53.770
in Montana history.

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:55.510
- [Male Reporter] An
unlikely leader in a state

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:59.300
where only half a percent of
the population looks like him.

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:04.300
- When I got elected, it
immediately became overwhelming,

00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:05.380
I have to tell you.

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:10.310
Within the first nine days,
we had 30 plus interviews

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:11.300
from everywhere.

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:14.050
So when Huffington Post called and said,

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:15.460
"How does it feel to make history?"

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:16.770
I said what history?

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:18.330
I was just running in
a small town election.

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:21.140
They said, "No, are you
aware you're the first

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:24.860
"African-American mayor in the history

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:26.160
"of the state of Montana."

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:28.730
I said, "No, I didn't think about it."

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:31.970
They said, and to crown it
off, you're a former refugee.

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:38.170
As time went on, I realized
it was more than just me.

00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:43.080
If I, Wilmot Collins, could
do that here in Helena,

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:46.150
others can see and say,
hey, we can emulate him.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:48.590
I had opened up the door.

00:02:51.234 --> 00:02:53.171
(door opening)

00:02:53.171 --> 00:02:54.004
(light switch clicking)

00:02:54.004 --> 00:02:56.300
The day-to-day is busy, no doubt.

00:02:57.500 --> 00:02:58.333
But,

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:00.133
it's rewarding.

00:03:02.937 --> 00:03:04.637
And I think the city's doing well.

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:11.630
Interacting with the
community is very important.

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:14.540
That's why the first
Wednesday of the month

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:17.550
I meet with the community,
I say, hey, talk to me.

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:18.383
What's going on?

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:22.080
How can we make this place a better place?

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:23.940
How can we continue to work together?

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:28.780
I'm pleased to say that my community

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:31.090
is one of the most accepting community

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:33.010
you can think about in this country.

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:40.080
A lot of people have
their own interpretation

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:41.610
of who a refugee is.

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:49.510
It's not fun fleeing your
country, fleeing what you know.

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:53.650
Going into a totally strange environment

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:54.950
that you know nothing about.

00:03:56.670 --> 00:03:58.950
Coming into this country, when I got in,

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:00.980
the first thing I started
hearing was people say,

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:03.590
"Oh, the immigrants, the refugees,

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:06.520
"they're gonna suck up our resources."

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:09.610
They're not gonna contribute
to this community.

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:10.443
It's not true.

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:13.560
We wanna give back to our host country.

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:14.950
We wanna give back to the country

00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:16.760
that gave us that second chance.

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:20.780
This is our country, and
we will do the best for it.

00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:24.739
Thanks for having me.

00:04:24.739 --> 00:04:26.989
(applause)

00:04:28.485 --> 00:04:30.902
(soft music)

00:04:32.351 --> 00:04:35.268
(electronic tones)

